        {
          "path": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0112.jpg",
          "filename": "Nan-hpaya, Pagan 0112.jpg",
          "caption": "This early temple dates from the reign of Anawrahta (\"ann-ARE-uh-tuh,\" 1044-1077), founder of the imperial dynasty at Pagan. \nArchitecturally, it is a gu temple, that is, a temple with a shrine. In this temple the shrine is preceeded by an entrance hall and topped with a sikhara, a plan which is similar to earlier Indian prototypes. A barrel-vaulted entrance hall leads into the shrine proper, that is surmounted by a small tower. The temple is constructed of brick, faced with soft sandstone. On later temples, stucco facing would be used instead of stone.",
          "alt": "This early temple dates from the reign of Anawrahta (\"ann-ARE-uh-tuh,\" 1044-1077), founder of the imperial dynasty at Pagan. \nArchitecturally, it is a gu temple, that is, a temple with a shrine. In this temple the shrine is preceeded by an entrance hall and topped with a sikhara, a plan which is similar to earlier Indian prototypes. A barrel-vaulted entrance hall leads into the shrine proper, that is surmounted by a small tower. The temple is constructed of brick, faced with soft sandstone. On later temples, stucco facing would be used instead of stone.",
          "width": 2360,
          "height": 1748,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
          "artist": "Michael Gunther",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nan-hpaya,_Pagan_0112.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "nanpaya-temple-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "w5g2",
          "mediumPath": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0112-medium.webp",
          "smallPath": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0112-small.webp"
        },
        {
          "path": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0111.jpg",
          "filename": "Nan-hpaya, Pagan 0111.jpg",
          "caption": "The goose (hamsa), Brahma's vehicle, is a frequently-appearing motif in the art of Pagan that was perhaps first used on the decoration of this temple. Rondels displaying the hamsa are carved along the \"waist\" of the temple above the base.",
          "alt": "The goose (hamsa), Brahma's vehicle, is a frequently-appearing motif in the art of Pagan that was perhaps first used on the decoration of this temple. Rondels displaying the hamsa are carved along the \"waist\" of the temple above the base.",
          "width": 2557,
          "height": 1851,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
          "artist": "Michael Gunther",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nan-hpaya,_Pagan_0111.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "nanpaya-temple-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "w5g2",
          "mediumPath": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0111-medium.webp",
          "smallPath": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0111-small.webp"
        },
        {
          "path": "_m/w/5/g/2/nanpaya-temple-wp/nan-hpaya-pagan-0110.jpg",
          "filename": "Nan-hpaya, Pagan 0110.jpg",
          "caption": "This is one of the windows on the shrine. In the tympanum (triangular area) above the perforated window is a kalasa pot filled with water and plants, symbolic of fertility. The arch is framed by two makaras that together make a double serpent rainbow, above which sits Sri Lakshmi, consort of Vishnu and goddess of good fortune and wealth. Considering these elements, according to Richard Cooler, the whole arch can be interpreted as an agrarian fertility symbol. \nThe downward-pointing triangle or \"V,\" on either side of the window, is another frequently-appearing motif at Pagan (for example, at Sulamani). It may also appear pointing upwards. It is usually filled with foliage, thus another auspicious symbol of nature's generative power.",
          "alt": "This is one of the windows on the shrine. In the tympanum (triangular area) above the perforated window is a kalasa pot filled with water and plants, symbolic of fertility. The arch is framed by two makaras that together make a double serpent rainbow, above which sits Sri Lakshmi, consort of Vishnu and goddess of good fortune and wealth. Considering these elements, according to Richard Cooler, the whole arch can be interpreted as an agrarian fertility symbol. \nThe downward-pointing triangle or \"V,\" on either side of the window, is another frequently-appearing motif at Pagan (for example, at Sulamani). It may also appear pointing upwards. It is usually filled with foliage, thus another auspicious symbol of nature's generative power.",
          "width": 1944,
          "height": 2592,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
          "artist": "Michael Gunther",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nan-hpaya,_Pagan_0110.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "nanpaya-temple-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "w5g2",
